    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that sounds pretty scary lol. is it common for companies to hide stuff like this in the fine print? like are there other \"sneaky\" things i should be looking out for in these kinds of contracts so i dont get screwed over later?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ha, yeah, it’s a bit of a scare tactic, but honestly, it’s pretty standard—companies love to put the heaviest liability on the other side, hoping you won’t read the fine print. Other sneaky things to watch for include **non-compete clauses** (which can restrict you from working with competitors for months after), **automatic renewal terms** (so you’re locked in without noticing), and **broad “assignment” rights** (letting them transfer the contract to another company without your consent). Also, keep an eye on **choice of law and venue**—if it says disputes happen in their state, you might have to travel far to fight a claim. And watch for **liquidated damages** clauses that set a fixed penalty for a breach, even if the actual harm is tiny. My advice: read every section, especially the “boilerplate” at the end—that’s where the real traps hide. And if you’re unsure, ask for a redline or a plain-English summary before signing.",
