    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You’re right to be wary—contracts are full of polite-sounding phrases that hide sharp teeth. Scan for words like **“notwithstanding”** (which overrides other sections), **“sole discretion”** (gives the other side total control), and **“irrevocable”** (means you can’t back out). Also flag **“indemnify”** (you pay their losses), **“joint and several liability”** (you’re on the hook for everyone’s mistakes), and **“material adverse change”** (lets them walk away if things go south). Watch for **“deemed accepted”** (silence means you agree) and **“survival”** clauses (obligations outlive the contract). And if you see **“reasonable”** attached to their actions, ask for specifics—it’s often a loophole for them to do whatever they want. The real trick is to read the *exceptions* and *footnotes*—that’s where the traps hide. When in doubt, ask for a “plain English” version or have a lawyer skim it. It’s worth the peace of mind.",
      "source": "target",
